From 0e5514a047fe18006c6072b73de92629f0a1ae0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:14:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NSDateFormatter: implement the settings macOS keeps

The settings a date formatter's keyed archive holds, compared with what
macOS 15 does with each:

- AMSymbol and PMSymbol could not be set.
- Symbols, leniency and the start of two digit years were lost whenever
  the ICU formatter was made again, on any change of locale, time zone,
  style or format; they are kept and set again.  Setting weekday symbols
  raised (ICU numbers weekdays from 1), and veryShortWeekdaySymbols
  were the short ones.
- A formatter is not lenient and two digit years start in 1950, as on
  macOS; ICU's defaults are lenient and 80 years before now.
  NSHTTPCookie read an Expires date such as "Wed, 09 Jun 2100" with the
  pattern "EEE, dd-MMM-yyyy" only because of that leniency, so it now
  asks for it.
- Not lenient on macOS is not ICU's strict parsing.  macOS calls
  udat_setLenient(false), which makes the calendar strict and turns off
  UDAT_PARSE_ALLOW_WHITESPACE and UDAT_PARSE_ALLOW_NUMERIC, and leaves
  UDAT_PARSE_PARTIAL_LITERAL_MATCH and
  UDAT_PARSE_MULTIPLE_PATTERNS_FOR_MATCH on; lenient turns all of them
  on.  That much GNUstep does too.  But Apple's ICU matches a literal
  differently when not lenient (APPLE_ICU_CHANGES, rdar://20692829, in
  SimpleDateFormat::matchLiterals): a character of the pattern that ICU
  would ignore before the next field ("-,./" or white space before a
  date field, "-.:" or white space before a time field, white space
  before another) matches any run of such characters in the text, or
  none before a letter.  So on macOS "yyyy-MM-dd" parses "1900/01/01",
  "1900.01.01" and "1900 01 01", "HH:mm" parses "10.30" and
  "dd.MM.yyyy" parses "01/01/1900", while "1900:01:01", "1900-13-01",
  "1900-02-30" and, with "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm", "1900-01-01 10:30" are
  refused.  ICU's own refuses the separators.  A strict parse that fails
  at a literal is now given the literal as Apple's ICU matches it there,
  and tried again.
- -dateFromString: took a date from any string that began with one
  ("1900-01-01x", "10:30 junk"); on macOS the string must be parsed to its
  end, or be followed only by white space other than line ends.
  -getObjectValue:forString:range:error: still takes what parses, and
  gives its length.
- defaultDate, gregorianStartDate and generatesCalendarDates did
  nothing.  What a string does not give now comes from the default date,
  or as on macOS from the start of 2000 in the formatter's time zone; the
  gregorian change is set on the calendar; parsing gives calendar dates
  when asked to.
- formattingContext is new, as ICU's capitalization context, and a keyed
  archive holds it as ICU's display context value, as macOS's does.
  Relative formatting uses the list item context, as on macOS ("Today").
- doesRelativeDateFormatting took effect only at the next change.
- -getObjectValue:forString:range:error: always failed.
- The default behaviour (0) parsed with the 10.0 calendar format.
  allowsNaturalLanguage is leniency with the 10.4 behaviour, and a 10.0
  formatter that allows natural language is lenient, as on macOS.
- GNUstep keeps a calendar in the locale; a keyed archive keeps them
  apart, as macOS's does.

Tests/base/NSDateFormatter/settings.m checks each against the values
macOS 15 gives, and that they survive a keyed archive; 35 of its 42
checks fail without the change.  Tests/base/NSDateFormatter/strictParsing.m
holds what macOS 15 gives for 159 formats and strings, with -dateFromString:
and -getObjectValue:forString:range:error:, lenient and not: separators,
white space, unpadded and out of range fields, two digit years, month names
for numbers and numbers for names, text before and after, partial strings
and time zones; 247 of its 318 checks fail without the change.
Six that differ for reasons other than these settings are left out: a
weekday alone (two), which macOS takes from the following week; two zone
names ICU here does not know; and two that only a lenient parse reads,
differently in each ICU ("0" as "dd", "1900-01-01" as "yyyyMMdd").

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
